  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nNo significant weather impacts are expected over the next week\nplus. The pattern will continue to be mainly dry with high \npressure dominating for the bulk of the forecast period. Only \nexceptions will be this afternoon and evening and again on \nThursday, as dry cold fronts push through the region. These \nfronts will bring an increase in wind gusts, however they will \nalso bring an increase in relative humidity, which alleviates \nsome of our fire weather concerns. Wind gusts of 15 to 20 mph \nwill be possible starting this afternoon as the pressure\ngradient over the region tightens some. Minimum RH will range \nin the upper 40s to low 50s percent in the mid afternoon, before\nincreasing in the late afternoon and evening as the stronger \nwind gusts arrive. Due to the increase in RH, we are not \nexpecting to issue any fire related special weather statements, \nbut will continue to monitor fire weather conditions closely.\n\nFor Tuesday, the winds will lighten up as high pressure moves\nback overhead. It will again be dry, with minimum relative\nhumidity dipping into the 30s percent range, but cooler\ntemperatures and the light winds should alleviate any fire\nweather concerns. \n\nThe cold front passing Thursday into Friday will have a small\nchance to produce some light showers or sprinkles across I90 \ncorridor. However, most of the region will remain dry and if any\nrain does occur across the north, it will be very light. \n\nHigh pressure will then return for this weekend. The next\npossible chance for rain will come either the end of the weekend\nor the beginning of next week. Models are in disagreement of the\noverall pattern next week, so there is no reason to stray from\nNBM for the long term forecast. At this time, the NBM is\nbringing low chances for rain starting Sunday night into Monday.",
